==Trivia==
*Prior to [[Version 4.7 Update|Version 4.7]], Rich Cat III was a part of another [[Cat Combo]] called [[Reinforcements (Removed Combo)|Reinforcements]].
*Prior to [[Version 14.1 Update|Version 14.1]], [[Sniper the Recruit (Super Rare Cat)|Sniper the Recruit]], Rich Cat III and [[Freshman Cat Jobs (Super Rare Cat)|Freshman Cat Jobs]] could only be unlocked when "Reinforcements" was part of the current pool.
*Rich Cat III's True Form evolution description initially came with a typo: "True '''Fom''' evolution gains greatly improved attack power!"''
*In Evolved and True Form, his knockback sprite resembles [[wikia:osomatsukun:Media:Tumblr nyuxjihUWx1v0jxj1o4 250.png|Iyami's "sheeh!" pose]] from ''Osomatsu-kun''.
*NekoSoft is likely to be a reference to the company [[wikipedia:Microsoft|Microsoft]].
*Rich Cat Sr. is a reference to Bakabon's Papa from [[wikipedia:Tensai Bakabon|''Tensai Bakabon'']]. In addition to wearing the same headband and bellyband that Bakabon's Papa is known for, Rich Cat Sr.'s Japanese description states that he is an unemployed father who is a living example of what not to do, which perfectly describes Bakabon's Papa.
**Rich Cat Sr.'s Japanese name is Nekobon's Papa. Nekobon is the [[Battle Items#Rich Cat|Rich Cat]]'s Japanese name, itself a portmanteau of ''neko'' (cat) and ''bonbon'' (young rich son), but it also serves as yet another allusion to Bakabon's Papa.
**[[Itchy Riches (Combo)|Itchy Riches]]' Japanese name, Nēēkobonbon, is a reference to the drawn-out "Bāākabonbon" from the anime's [https://youtu.be/9XwOi-G3idE opening theme].
*In Richest Cat's Japanese description, "猫川埋蔵金" (''Nekogawa Maizōkin'') is a reference to the large amount of treasure that the Tokugawa shogunate is believed to have hidden at the end of the Edo period in Japan (徳川埋蔵金, ''Tokugawa Maizōkin'').

